{"name":"Altar of Heaven","hex":"#4d4c80","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"altar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A table or similar flat-topped structure used for religious rites.","A raised area around an altar in a church; the sanctuary."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/altar"},{"term":"heaven","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sky, specifically.","The abode of God or the gods, traditionally conceived as beyond the sky; especially."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To transport to the abode of God, the gods, or the blessed.","To beatify, enchant, or please greatly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/heaven"}],"entries":[{"title":"Santa Maria in Ara Coeli","blocks":[{"text":"The Basilica of Saint Mary of the Altar in Heaven is a titular basilica and conventual church of the Franciscan Convent of Aracoeli located on the highest summit of the Capitoline Hill in Rome, Italy. From 1250 to 1798 it was the headquarters of the General Curia of the Order of Friars Minor as well as being one of the city's principal civic churches. It is still the designated church of the city council of Rome, which uses the ancient title of Senatus Populusque Romanus. The present cardinal priest of the Titulus Sanctæ Mariæ de Aracœli is Salvatore De Giorgi."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg/330px-Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Maria_in_Ara_Coeli","wikidata":"Q318660"},{"title":"Temple of Heaven","blocks":[{"text":"The Temple of Heaven is a complex of imperial religious Confucian buildings situated in the southeastern part of central Beijing. The complex was visited by the Emperors of the Ming and then later restored by the Qing dynasties for annual ceremonies of prayer to Heaven for a good harvest and other seasonal rites. The Temple of Heaven was inscribed as a World Heritage site in 1998 and was described as \"a masterpiece of architecture and landscape design which simply and graphically illustrates a cosmogony of great importance for the evolution of one of the world's great civilizations...\" as the \"symbolic layout and design of the Temple of Heaven had a profound influence on architecture and planning in the Far East over many centuries.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Temple_of_Heaven_20160323_01.jpg/330px-Temple_of_Heaven_20160323_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Heaven","wikidata":"Q125445"},{"title":"Altar","blocks":[{"text":"An altar is a table or platform for the presentation of religious offerings, for sacrifices, or for other ritualistic purposes. Altars are found at shrines, temples, churches, and other places of worship. They are used particularly in Christianity, Buddhism, Hinduism, and modern paganism. Many historical-medieval faiths also made use of them, including the Roman, Greek, and Norse religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/RD_alter.jpg/330px-RD_alter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altar","wikidata":"Q101687"},{"title":"Heaven","blocks":[{"text":"Heaven, or the Heavens, is a common religious cosmological or supernatural place where beings such as deities, angels, souls, saints, or venerated ancestors are said to originate, be enthroned, or reside. According to the beliefs of some religions, heavenly beings can descend to Earth or incarnate and earthly beings can ascend to Heaven in the afterlife or, in exceptional cases, enter Heaven without dying."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Paradiso_Canto_31_%28148200393%29.jpg/330px-Paradiso_Canto_31_%28148200393%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heaven","wikidata":"Q4489450"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg/500px-Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg/1280px-Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg/500px-Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_%28Rome%29_-_Facade.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASanta_Maria_in_Aracoeli_(Rome)_-_Facade.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/01_Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_Nave.jpg/500px-01_Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_Nave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/01_Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_Nave.jpg/1280px-01_Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_Nave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/01_Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_Nave.jpg/500px-01_Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_Nave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Interior of the church","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A01_Santa_Maria_in_Aracoeli_Nave.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Cavallini_fresco_-_Aracoeli_-_antmoose_-_cropped.jpg/500px-Cavallini_fresco_-_Aracoeli_-_antmoose_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb